<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottavio Farnese</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1524">1524</a>–<a href="/years/1586">1586</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ottavio Farnese was duke of Parma and Piacenza from 1547, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a>. After his father Pier Luigi was murdered, he held on to Parma against imperial pressure, fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-parma">War of Parma</a> with French support in 1551–1552. Through his marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-parma">Margaret of Parma</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, he was reconciled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, and Philip II returned Piacenza to him in 1556.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ottavio-farnese.jpg" alt="Ottavio Farnese" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maso da San Friano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tommaso_Manzuoli,_called_Maso_da_San_Friano_(1531%E2%80%931571)_-_Ottavio_Farnese_(1525-86),_2nd_Duke_of_Parma_and_Piacenza_-_CC446_-_Cobbe_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ottavio Farnese was born on 9 October 1524 in Valentano, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pier-luigi-farnese">Pier Luigi Farnese</a>, Duke of Parma and Piacenza, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a>, and of Gerolama Orsini. His brother was Cardinal Ranuccio Farnese. On 4 November 1538 he married Margaret, the illegitimate daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor</a>. He was 14 and she was 15 and recently widowed by the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-de-medici">Alessandro de' Medici</a>. She at first disliked her youthful husband, but when he returned wounded from an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1541 her aversion turned to affection. He became Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camerino">Camerino</a> in 1540 and gave up that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> when his father became Duke of Parma in 1545.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the Parmesan nobility assassinated Pier Luigi in 1547, imperial troops occupied Piacenza. Paul III set aside Ottavio's claim to succeed in Parma, appointed a papal legate there and gave Ottavio Camerino in exchange, then claimed Piacenza from the emperor for the Church rather than for the Farnese. Ottavio tried to seize Parma by force, failed, and negotiated with Ferrante Gonzaga. This rebellion is believed to have hastened the pope's death on 10 November 1549. He tried again during the interregnum, again without success, but on the election of Julius III the duchy was conferred on him in 1551.</p><p>The quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> was not ended, for Gonzaga refused to give up Piacenza and threatened to occupy Parma, and Ottavio was driven towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Julius III, anxious for good relations with Charles while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> was sitting, ordered him to hand Parma back to the papal authorities. On his refusal he deprived Ottavio of his Roman fiefs, and Charles did the same with those in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>. A French army came to protect Parma, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-parma">War of Parma</a> broke out and Gonzaga besieged the city. Ottavio then came to an arrangement with his father-in-law, by which he regained Piacenza and his other fiefs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The rest of his life was spent quietly at home, where the moderation and wisdom of his rule are said to have won him the affection of his people. His first son, Charles, born on 27 August 1545, died the same year. A second son, Alexander, born on the same day, became the third Duke of Parma. Ottavio died on 18 September 1586 and Alexander succeeded him. He also had two daughters, Violante and Ersilia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ottavio Farnese, Duke of Parma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Ottavio Farnese</b> held on to Parma, with French support in the War of Parma of 1551–1552, and through his marriage to Margaret of Parma, the illegitimate daughter of Charles V, he reconciled the family with the Habsburgs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Valentano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Parma, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.57,11.83&amp;year=1564" aria-label="Valentano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4836 651 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ottavio Farnese"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5286 921h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5286 921h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.57,11.83&amp;year=1564">What was here in 1564? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
